# Tutorial 24 — Layers, Display Slots and Lighting
# Reference: docs/scripting/vns/presentation/vns-movable-layer-groups.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_24
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: My character ID, expression, display slot, layer group, and lighting preset solve different problems. Keeping them distinct makes complex staging manageable.
@label lesson_menu
> Inspect layered expressions -> expressions
> Slots, groups and eye focus -> groups
> Stage lighting workflow -> lighting
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label expressions
[show lavender center idle]
Lavender: Idle uses a body, eyes, and mouth. Open {b}scripts/definitions/characters.vns{/b} to inspect the actual declarations in your project.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Happy changes the selected parts. The unchanged source art is reused.
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: Emphasis selects another combination. A layered preset can create many expressions from a small asset set.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Use the Layered Image Visualizer and image attribute tools to inspect alignment and transparent margins. Keep layers on the same coordinate canvas.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label groups
Lavender: Display slots let a character occupy independently addressable stage instances. Presets can reuse a blocking arrangement without redefining the character.
Lavender: Movable layer groups collect parts such as an arm, head, or face so a timeline can transform them together. Declare layers and groups before presets that reference them.
Lavender: Eye focus maps named eye layers and small offsets to a target. It complements expressions; it does not replace your character's identity or the active slot.
Lavender: Exercise: create a face group in a copy of the character file, animate a small group movement in Puppeteer, and check that the outline effect stays attached to each rendered layer.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label lighting
Lavender: Scene Lighting Studio exports a {b}.stagepreset{/b} file. Declare it with @stagepreset and select it with the stage command.
narrator: @stagepreset warm config/stage/warm.stagepreset
narrator: [stage warm]
narrator: [stage clear]
Lavender: These are authoring examples; no lighting preset is applied to our plain white workshop. Build a separate lighting test scene when you want to grade the background.
Lavender: Lights can target different scene layers. Occluders and response zones describe how light reaches surfaces; named presets carry that setup into VNS and Puppeteer.
Lavender: Exercise: light a character, move it through the light, switch its expression, then clear the preset. Check the result with and without line boil.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: Which part decides the combination of eyes and mouth currently drawn?
> The character expression or layered preset -> correct
> The plugin's API version range -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: API compatibility controls whether a plugin can load. The expression chooses the visual parts.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Exactly. Separate content, placement, transformation, and rendering effects.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_24]
[flag tutorial_done_24]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_5]
